<Docs title="Language Fundamentals" description="The core shape of a RUDRA program: entry point, statements and comments." slug="language-fundamentals">
  <p class="lead">A RUDRA program is a set of functions. Execution begins at <code>main</code>.</p>

  <h2>Program structure</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={`fn main() {
    print("Hello, RUDRA!");
}`} />

  <h2>Statements</h2>
  <p>Statements end with a semicolon. Bindings, assignments, function calls and control-flow blocks make up the body of a function.</p>

  <h2>Comments</h2>
  <p>RUDRA supports line and block comments.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={`fn main() {
    print("start");
    print("end");
}`} />
  <p>In your own source you may write <code>// line comment</code> and <code>/* block comment */</code>. This documentation keeps examples comment-free for clarity.</p>

  <h2>Printing</h2>
  <p>The built-in <code>print</code> writes a value followed by a newline. It accepts strings, integers, floats and booleans.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={`fn main() {
    print("text");
    print(42);
    print(3.14);
    print(true);
}`} />
</Docs>
